<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="s_natural_numbers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Natural numbers: Peano axioms</title>

    <theorem xml:id="th_peano">
    <title>Peano Axioms</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    There is a set <m>\N</m> satisfying the following properties. 
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
                There is a function <m>S\colon \N\rightarrow \N</m> defined on <m>\N</m> called the <term>successor function</term>. 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                There is an element <m>0\in \N</m> such that <m>0\ne S(n)</m> for all <m>n\in \N</m>. 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                The successor function is injective: i.e., for all <m>m,n\in \N</m>, if <m>S(m)=S(n)</m>, then <m>m=n</m>. Using logical shorthand, this means 
                <md>
                    m\ne n \implies S(m)\ne S(n)
                </md>,                
                or equivalently, 
                <md>
                    S(m)=S(n) \implies m=n
                </md>
                for all <m>m,n\in \N</m>. 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Induction principle</title>
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is a subset of <m>\N</m> satisfying 
                <ol marker="(i)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>0\in A</m>, and 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            for all <m>n\in \N</m>, if <m>n\in A</m>, then <m>S(n)\in A</m>,
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            then <m>A=\N</m>. 
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
    </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
    <p>
    </p>
    </proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>
        Once we have a set <m>\N</m> satisfying the properties of <xref ref="th_peano"/>, called the <term>Peano axioms</term>, we can recognize our old friend <m>\{0,1,2,\dots, \}</m> by introducing the notation <m>S(n)=n+1</m> for all <m>n\in \N</m>, and adding some additional names of elements resulting from successive application of <m>S</m> starting with <m>0</m>: <eg/>,
        <md>
            <mrow>1 \amp =S(0)=0+1 \amp 2\amp =S(1)=1+1 \amp 3\amp=S(2)=2+1</mrow>
            <mrow>4 \amp = S(3)=3+1 \amp 5\amp=S(4)=4+1 \amp 6\amp=S(5)=5+1</mrow>
            <mrow>7 \amp = S(6)=6+1 \amp 8\amp=S(7)=7+1 \amp 9\amp=S(8)=8+1</mrow>
        </md>.
        Next with the help of the induction principle, we can define not just a successor function, but further a full-blown addition operation 
        <md>
            <mrow>+\colon \N\times\N \amp \rightarrow \N</mrow>
            <mrow>(m,n) \amp\mapsto m+n</mrow>
        </md>.
        We will not do this in detail, as it a tad technical (involving a further <em>recursive principle</em>), but roughly speaking <m>m+n</m> is defined as applying the successor function <m>n</m> times to <m>m</m>: <ie/>, 
        <md>
            m+n=\underset{n \text{ times}}{\underbrace{S(S(\dots S(m)))}}
        </md>.
        At this point we are well on our way to reconstructing the usual arithmetic on <m>\N</m>; we can give a similar type of definition for the multiplication operation 
        <md>
            (m,n)\mapsto m\cdot n
        </md>.
        Next we can construct <m>\Z</m> as 
        <m></m>
        
        
    </p>
   <corollary xml:id="cor_generalized_induction">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>a\in \Z</m>, and let <m>A</m> be a subset of <m>\Z_{\geq a}</m>. If <m>A</m> satisfies 
            <ol marker="(i)">
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>a\in A</m>, and 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        for all <m>n\geq a</m>, if <m>n\in A</m>, then <m>n+1\in A</m>,
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        then <m>A=\Z_{\geq a}</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
   </corollary>
    <p>
    The fourth property of <xref ref="th_peano"/> directly gives rise to a proof technique for showing that a given subset <m>A</m> of <m>\N</m> is actually all of <m>\N</m>: namely, show that (i) <m>0\in \N</m>, and (ii) show that for all <m>n\in \N</m>, if <m>n\in A</m>, then <m>n+1\in A</m>. As stated, this is a proof technique for showing an <em>equality of sets</em> (<m>A=\N</m>). It is useful, however to give an equivalent formulation of this principle that yields a technique for proving a technique for proving universal quantifications of the form
    <md>
      \text{For all integers } n\geq b, \, P(n)
    </md>,
    where <m>b</m> is a fixed starting integer, called the <term>base</term>, and <m>P</m> is a predicate defined on the integers. Using logical shorthand, we seek a proof technique for showing that propositions of the form 
    <md>
        \forall n\in \Z_{\geq b}\  P(n)
    </md>.
    The technique in question is called <term>proof by induction</term>. 
   </p> 
   <algorithm xml:id="proof_by_induction">
   <title>Proof by induction</title>
   <statement>
   <p>
   Let <m>b</m> be an integer, and let <m>P(x)</m> be a predicate defined on all integers <m>n\geq b</m>. To prove the proposition
   <md>
        \forall n\in \Z_{\geq b}\  P(n)
    </md>, 
 proceed as follows. 
    <ul>
        <li>
            <title>Base step</title>
            <p>
                Prove that <m>P(b)</m> is true. 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Induction step</title>
            <p>
                Prove that for all integers <m>n\geq b</m>, if <m>P(n)</m> is true, then <m>P(n+1)</m> is true. Using logical: prove the proposition
                <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_induction_implication">
                  \forall n\geq b \  P(n)\implies P(n+1)
                </md>.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
   </p>
   </statement>
   </algorithm>
   <example xml:id="eg_induction_sum_of_integers">
      <title>Induction: sum of first <m>n</m> integers</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove the identity
          <md>
            \sum_{k=1}^n k=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}
          </md>
          for all <m>n\geq 1</m>. Recall:
          <md>
            \sum_{k=1}^n k=1+2+\cdots +n
          </md>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We prove the proposition by induction.
        </p>
        <case>
         <title>Step 0: preparation</title>
        <p>
          The proposition is modeled logically as <m>\forall n\geq 1 \, P(n)</m>, where <m>P(n)</m> is the proposition that
          <md>
            \sum_{k=1}^n k=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}
          </md>.
        </p>
        </case>
        <case>
         <title>Base step: <m>n=1</m></title>
        <p>
        The proposition <m>P(1)</m> is the statement that
        <md>
          1=\frac{1(1+1)}{2}
        </md>,
        which is true since 
      <md>
        \frac{1(1+1)}{2}=\frac{2}{2}=1
      </md>.
        </p>
        </case>
        <case>
         <title>Induction step</title>
        <p>
        We must show the universal implication
        <md>
          \forall n\in \Z_{\geq 1}\ P(n)\implies P(n+1)
        </md>.
        Let <m>n\geq 1</m>, and assume <m>P(n)</m> is true: <ie />,
        <md>
          \sum_{k=1}^n k=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}
        </md>
        The proposition <m>P(n+1)</m> states that
        <md>
          \sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k=\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}
        </md>. We prove this, assuming <m>P(n)</m>, via a chain of equalities:
        <md>
          <mrow> \sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k\amp= \sum_{k=1}^{n} k+(n+1) </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp=\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+n+1\amp (\text{induction hyp.}) </mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp =\frac{n(n+1)+2(n+1)}{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>  \amp =\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}{2}</mrow>
        </md>,
        as desired.
        </p>
        </case>
        <p>
          Having completed our base and induction steps, our proof is now finished.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
   <algorithm xml:id="proof_by_strong_induction">
   <title>Proof by strong induction</title>
   <statement>
   <p>
   Let <m>b</m> be an integer, and let <m>P(x)</m> be a predicate defined on all integers <m>n\geq b</m>. To prove the proposition
   <md>
        \forall n\in \Z_{\geq b}\  P(n)
    </md>, 
 proceed as follows. 
    <ul>
        <li>
            <title>Base step</title>
            <p>
                Prove that <m>P(b)</m> is true. 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <title>Strong induction step</title>
            <p>
                Prove that for all integers <m>n\geq b</m>, if <m>P(x)</m> is true for all integers <m>b\leq x\leq n</m>, then <m>P(n+1)</m> is true. Using logical: prove the proposition
                <md number="yes" xml:id="eq_strong_induction_implication">
   \forall n\geq b\, \left(P(b)\land P(b+1)\cdots \land P(n)\right) \implies P(n+1)
 </md>.  
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
   </p>
   </statement>
   </algorithm>
   
    <theorem xml:id="th_well-ordering">
    <title>Well-ordering princple</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    Let <m>b</m> be an integer. Given any nonempty subset <m>A</m> of <m>\Z_{\geq b}</m>, <m>A</m> has a least element: <ie/>, there is an element <m>a\in A</m> such that <m>a\leq a'</m> for all <m>a'\in A</m>. 
    </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
    <p>
        This is left as a homework exercise. 
    </p>
    </proof>
    </theorem>


</section>